<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <RootNamespace>FrameLink.Agent</RootNamespace>
    <!-- System register (§1.1): the binary, the unit and every route are `fl-agent`. -->
    <AssemblyName>fl-agent</AssemblyName>
    <Version>0.1.0</Version>

    <!--
      The version this binary reports is a wire value (§2.8: the updater matches it against the
      served version, never compares). IncludeSourceRevisionInInformationalVersion=false is what
      stops the SDK decorating it, and it is set in Directory.Build.props rather than here —
      deliberately, because build.sh passes -p:InformationalVersion as a global property that
      reaches every referenced project, and a project still carrying the decorated string gets it
      linked into this same AOT binary. build.sh fails the build (exit 7) if any version string in
      the ELF is decorated; AgentVersionIsExactlyWhatTheBuildAskedFor in AgentUpdateTests is the
      guard on the value this program actually reports.
    -->

    <!-- §2.1 "One single Native AOT binary. No supplemental program files, ever."
         Publishing is done from the emulated linux-arm64 container (§5.2); this
         property is what makes that publish AOT, and what keeps the trim/AOT
         analysers honest about the code in between. -->
    <PublishAot>true</PublishAot>
    <StripSymbols>true</StripSymbols>
    <InvariantGlobalization>true</InvariantGlobalization>

    <!-- Deliberately NOT set: UseSystemResourceKeys. It would shrink the binary by
         replacing framework exception messages with bare resource ids — directly
         against the transparency principle (§1.2.3), since those messages are what
         the journal and the console stage have to render when something breaks. -->

    <!-- The console stage measures the real panel through ioctl(TIOCGWINSZ); the
         LibraryImport source generator emits unsafe marshalling code for it. -->
    <AllowUnsafeBlocks>true</AllowUnsafeBlocks>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- §2.1 again: the unit file is not a supplemental program file shipped beside
         the binary, it lives *inside* it and is written out by `fl-agent install`. -->
    <EmbeddedResource Include="Systemd\fl-agent.service" LogicalName="fl-agent.service" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    §2.1: "The product app is embedded too. The v1 SPA service and its on-disk git
    checkout are gone; the agent serves the app from its own binary, so the app can
    never drift from the agent managing it, and the repair screen and product share
    one local origin."

    LogicalName has to be set explicitly. Without it the SDK manufactures
    `fl-agent.app.vendor.lit-all.min.js` — every separator collapsed into a dot, so a
    request path can no longer be recovered from the resource name at all. The
    String.Replace call normalises %(RecursiveDir), which carries the *host's*
    separator: a build on the workstation would otherwise embed `app/vendor\lit.js`
    and the arm64 container `app/vendor/lit.js`, and the served URL would depend on
    where the binary was built. EmbeddedApp normalises again on read, so neither form
    can reach a URL.

    config.example.json is excluded on purpose. The five values it templates are now
    Fleet-Manager-supplied and served from /config.json (LocalOrigin); shipping the
    example beside them would give the app a second, stale place to find them.

    WithCulture="false" is not decoration. MSBuild's AssignCulture target reads the segment
    before the final extension as a culture name, so a file added here as `panel.de.js` would be
    taken for German, routed to the satellite-assembly path, have its LogicalName discarded and
    be renamed - and the build would succeed, warn about nothing, and still list the item as
    present and correct under -getItem:EmbeddedResource. The frame would simply not have the
    file. This was measured on the vendored firmware block below, where a `.bin.gz` name was
    eaten exactly that way; this block escaped only because no filename here has yet had a
    segment that parses as a culture. Naming luck is not a guard, so the guard is written down.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <EmbeddedResource Include="..\..\app\**\*.*"
                      Exclude="..\..\app\config.example.json;..\..\app\config.json;..\..\app\.gitignore"
                      WithCulture="false">
      <LogicalName>app/$([System.String]::Copy('%(RecursiveDir)').Replace('\','/'))%(Filename)%(Extension)</LogicalName>
    </EmbeddedResource>
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    Decision 91's other half: "a frame that has an agent has the firmware". The DFU images
    vendored under vendor/respeaker-xvf3800/ travel inside this binary, so the one operation
    on a frame that cannot be undone depends on nothing outside the executable performing it.
    vendor/respeaker-xvf3800/NOTICE.md records where the bytes came from and what they hash to.

    Same LogicalName transform as app/** above, for the same reason and not a second mechanism:
    without it the SDK manufactures `fl-agent.vendor.respeaker-xvf3800.<file>.bin`, and
    %(RecursiveDir) carries the *host's* separator, so a build on the workstation and a build in
    the arm64 container would embed different resource names — a difference that would surface
    only at runtime, on a frame, at the moment somebody wanted to flash. XvfVendoredFirmware
    normalises again on read, so neither form can reach a lookup.

    A glob rather than one named file, deliberately: the v2.0.6 fallback and the 4mb_all_ff
    erase image are pinned but NOT vendored, and whether they join this directory is an open
    question. If they do, they are a `git add` and a ledger entry — not an edit here. Everything
    below is written per-item so that adding a file changes no line of this project.

    Only *.bin. NOTICE.md sits beside them and is documentation, not payload.

    COMPRESSED, AND BY THE BUILD RATHER THAN IN THE REPOSITORY. A managed resource is stored
    verbatim: measured 2026-08-24, embedding this image raw put all 933,888 of its bytes contiguously
    into the linux-arm64 ELF at offset 7,139,897, byte for byte, and the binary grew 983,448. That is
    the better part of a megabyte every frame in the fleet pulls over §2.8's hourly update feed on
    every release, for a file that gzip -9 takes to 300,528. So a .gz is what is embedded, and the
    decompression happens on the way to the card.

    Compressed HERE and not committed compressed, which is the load-bearing half: the file in
    vendor/ has to stay the bytes upstream served, because that is the entire claim NOTICE.md makes
    and the only thing a reader can re-derive with curl and sha256sum. A committed .gz would be a
    second artifact nobody can check against upstream without first trusting this project's
    compressor. The .gz is therefore intermediate output, regenerated from the vendored bytes by
    the target below, and never committed.

    GZIP AND NOT BROTLI, WHICH IS A BUILD-TOOLING LIMIT AND NOT A PREFERENCE. Brotli-11 takes the
    same image to 234,707 — 65,821 better, 0.6% of the binary. It is not reachable from an inline
    task: RoslynCodeTaskFactory compiles the fragment below against a netstandard2.0 reference set,
    which has System.IO.Compression but no BrotliStream (.NET Core 2.1+). Naming the shared-framework
    assembly gets BrotliStream and then dies on CS0012 for `Stream`, because its base type forwards
    to System.Private.CoreLib, which cannot be referenced; naming the reference assemblies instead
    replaces System.Runtime under the factory's own reference set and loses `System.Void`. Both were
    tried. Buying those 65,821 bytes therefore means a real task assembly or a compressor in the
    build image — a standing cost in every build, on both platforms, and the operator's call rather
    than a thing to slip in. GZipStream needs nothing at all and delivers 67.8% of the saving.

    Nothing about verification moves. XvfVendoredFirmware hands VerifiedFetch a decompressing
    stream, and VerifiedFetch checks the same length and the same SHA-256 against the same pin it
    checks a download against. Compression makes both checks stricter rather than weaker: the digest
    is now computed over bytes that were *reconstructed* rather than merely copied, and the length
    bound is the ceiling on a decompression bomb.

    An inline task rather than an `Exec` on a `gzip` binary: the arm64 build image carries clang,
    zlib and binutils, the test suite builds this same project on Windows where there is no gzip at
    all, and a build that compresses on one platform and not the other is the class of difference
    that only shows up at run time. The task runs inside MSBuild, which is the same .NET that
    compiles this project, everywhere.
  -->
  <UsingTask TaskName="GzipCompressFile"
             TaskFactory="RoslynCodeTaskFactory"
             AssemblyFile="$(MSBuildToolsPath)\Microsoft.Build.Tasks.Core.dll">
    <ParameterGroup>
      <Source ParameterType="System.String" Required="true" />
      <Destination ParameterType="System.String" Required="true" />
    </ParameterGroup>
    <Task>
      <Using Namespace="System.IO" />
      <Using Namespace="System.IO.Compression" />
      <Code Type="Fragment" Language="cs">
        <![CDATA[
          // (CompressionLevel)3 is SmallestSize, which GZipStream maps to zlib level 9 and which is
          // what the 933,888 -> 300,528 above was measured at. Written as a cast rather than by name
          // because this fragment compiles against netstandard2.0, whose CompressionLevel stops at
          // Fastest; the value is what reaches the real enum at run time. Optimal would NOT do — it
          // is zlib level 6 and gives up about 5% of the saving for build time nobody is short of.
          Directory.CreateDirectory(Path.GetDirectoryName(Destination));
          using (var source = File.OpenRead(Source))
          using (var destination = File.Create(Destination))
          using (var gzip = new GZipStream(destination, (CompressionLevel)3, false))
          {
              source.CopyTo(gzip);
          }
        ]]>
      </Code>
    </Task>
  </UsingTask>

  <ItemGroup>
    <VendoredFirmware Include="..\..\vendor\respeaker-xvf3800\**\*.bin">
      <!-- The host's separator is right here: this is a real path on the machine building. -->
      <Compressed>$(IntermediateOutputPath)vendored-firmware\%(RecursiveDir)%(Filename)%(Extension).gz</Compressed>
      <!-- And normalised here, because this one becomes a lookup key inside the binary. The `.gz`
           is part of the name on purpose: the stored bytes are not a DFU image, and a resource whose
           name says otherwise is how the next reader gets hurt. -->
      <LogicalName>firmware/$([System.String]::Copy('%(RecursiveDir)').Replace('\','/'))%(Filename)%(Extension).gz</LogicalName>
      <!--
        WithCulture=false, and this one is not defensive: without it the build silently produces a
        binary with no firmware in it. SplitResourcesByCulture runs AssignCulture over every
        EmbeddedResource, which reads the second-to-last extension as a culture name — and in
        `respeaker_xvf3800_usb_dfu_firmware_v2.1.0.bin.gz` that is `bin`. MSBuild says so in its own
        words at detailed verbosity: `Culture of "bin" was assigned`. The item is then routed down
        the satellite-assembly path, LogicalName is discarded, the resource is renamed to
        `FrameLink.Agent.obj.Release.net10._0.vendored_firmware.…` and never reaches the main
        assembly. Nothing warns. The build succeeds, -getItem:EmbeddedResource shows the item
        present and correct, and the only symptom is a frame that cannot find its own firmware.

        The app/** block above is exposed to exactly the same trap and escapes it only by luck of
        naming — `lit-all.min.js` survives because `min` is not a culture. A file added there as
        `something.de.js` would vanish the same way.
      -->
      <WithCulture>false</WithCulture>
    </VendoredFirmware>
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    Two targets rather than one, and the EmbeddedResource added HERE rather than in the ItemGroup
    above, which is not a style choice: Microsoft.NET.Sdk.DefaultItems.targets is imported at the
    BOTTOM of this project and ends with `<EmbeddedResource Remove="$(BaseIntermediateOutputPath)/**">`.
    An item declared at evaluation time that points into obj/ is therefore silently removed after
    this file has been read — the item dumps fine under -getItem:EmbeddedResource, the .gz is written
    where it should be, the build succeeds, and the resource is simply absent from the assembly.
    Adding it from a target puts it in after every Remove has already run.

    BeforeTargets=AssignTargetPaths because that is the first link in PrepareResourceNamesDependsOn:
    by PrepareResources the manifest names are already assigned, and BeforeTargets on any later
    target in that chain runs after its own DependsOnTargets and so arrives too late.

    Inputs/Outputs on the compression half only. It is what makes an incremental build skip
    re-compressing a megabyte; the item still has to be added on every build, which is why the two
    halves cannot be one target.
  -->
  <Target Name="_CompressVendoredFirmware"
          Inputs="@(VendoredFirmware)"
          Outputs="@(VendoredFirmware->'%(Compressed)')">
    <GzipCompressFile Source="%(VendoredFirmware.FullPath)" Destination="%(VendoredFirmware.Compressed)" />
  </Target>

  <Target Name="EmbedVendoredFirmware"
          BeforeTargets="AssignTargetPaths"
          DependsOnTargets="_CompressVendoredFirmware">
    <!-- Metadata travels through an item transform, so Compressed and LogicalName arrive with it. -->
    <ItemGroup>
      <EmbeddedResource Include="@(VendoredFirmware->'%(Compressed)')" />
    </ItemGroup>
  </Target>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\FrameLink.Protocol\FrameLink.Protocol.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
